  "nl_text": "The schemabench_0149 database is used for a subscription-based service that allows users to access features and services based on their subscription plan. The database contains nine tables, each representing a different entity or relationship in the system.\nThe subscription table contains information about each subscription, including the account ID, plan, status, start and end dates, and Stripe subscription ID. The table also has foreign keys to the account and user tables, which are used to link the subscription to the user and account that it belongs to.\nThe audit_log table contains information about any changes made to the system, including the user who made the change, the account affected, and the action taken. The table also has foreign keys to the account and user tables, which are used to link the audit log entry to the user and account that it affects.\nThe account table contains information about each account, including the email address, date created, Stripe customer ID, Stripe subscription ID, plan, referrer, and whether the account is active. The table also has foreign keys to the user and invite tables, which are used to link the account to the user who created it and any invites that have been sent to the account.\nThe user table contains information about each user, including the name, email address, password, date created, last login, permission, push token, and account ID. The table also has foreign keys to the account and session tables, which are used to link the user to the account they belong to and any sessions they have started.\nThe feature table contains information about each feature, including the name, description, and date created. The table also has a unique constraint on the name column to ensure that each feature has a unique name.\nThe invite table contains information about each invite, including the invite ID, email address, account ID, date sent, and whether the invite has been used. The table also has a foreign key to the account table, which is used to link the invite to the account it belongs to.\nThe session table contains information about each session, including the user ID, session token, date created, expiration date, and whether the session is still active. The table also has a foreign key to the user table, which is used to link the session to the user who started it.\nThe plan_feature table contains information about the features that are included in each plan, including the plan name and feature ID. The table also has a foreign key to the feature table, which is used to link the plan to the features it includes.\nThe payment table contains information about each payment, including the account ID, amount, currency, payment date, status, and Stripe payment ID. The table also has a foreign key to the account table, which is used to link the payment to the account it belongs to.\nOverall, the schemabench_0149 database is designed to support the subscription-based service by storing information about subscriptions, accounts, users, features, invites, sessions, and payments. The database uses foreign keys and constraints to ensure data integrity and consistency, and it is designed to be easily scalable and flexible to support future changes and updates.",
  "gold_question": "1) Background / Context\n\nGravity is a Software as a Service (SaaS) boilerplate designed to expedite the development of SaaS applications. It leverages Node.js for the backend and React for the frontend. The boilerplate offers a comprehensive framework that includes user authentication, subscription management, and AI integrations with ChatGPT and DALL-E, all supported by a REST API compatible with multiple databases. \n\n2) Objectives\n\nThe primary objective of Gravity is to provide a robust and flexible starting point for developers to build custom SaaS solutions quickly. By incorporating essential SaaS features, the platform aims to reduce development time and effort, allowing developers to focus on their application's unique aspects.\n\n3) Stakeholders & Users\n\n- Developers: Utilize the boilerplate for developing custom SaaS applications.\n- End Users: Benefit indirectly through the applications built using Gravity, involving user authentication and personalized services.\n- Administrators: Manage user roles, access controls, and oversee the application's functionalities.\n\n4) Functional Descriptions\n\n- User Authentication: Supports user registration, login, and session management.\n- Subscription Management: Integrates with Stripe for handling subscription plans and payments.\n- Role-based Access Control: Provides granular access permissions based on user roles.\n- AI Integrations: Integrates ChatGPT and DALL-E for AI-enhanced functionalities within applications.\n- REST API: Offers a fast, efficient API for database interactions and system operations.\n\n5) Non-Functional Descriptions\n\n- Code Quality: Emphasizes a well-documented, high-quality codebase for ease of use and maintenance.\n- Performance: Designed to efficiently support multiple databases and ensure quick response times.\n- Security: Ensures secure payment processing and data integrity across the system.",
  "input_json": "/data/SchemaBench/Experiment/schema2text_codellama13b/0149.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0149\nCREATE TABLE `subscription` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `account_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `plan` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `status` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `start_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `end_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `stripe_subscription_id` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `subscription_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`account_id`) REFERENCES `account` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `subscription_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `user` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `audit_log` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `account_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `action` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `timestamp` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `audit_log_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `user` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `audit_log_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`account_id`) REFERENCES `account` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `account` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `date_created` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `stripe_customer_id` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `stripe_subscription_id` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `plan` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `referrer` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `is_active` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT '1',\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `account_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `user` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `account_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`invite_id`) REFERENCES `invite` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `user` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `password` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `date_created` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `last_login` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `permission` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `push_token` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `account_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `user_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`account_id`) REFERENCES `account` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `user_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`session_id`) REFERENCES `session` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `feature` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL UNIQUE,\n  `description` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  `date_created` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `invite` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `account_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `date_sent` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `is_used` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT '0',\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `invite_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`account_id`) REFERENCES `account` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `session` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `session_token` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `date_created` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `expiration_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `is_active` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT '1',\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `session_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `user` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `plan_feature` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `plan_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `feature_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `plan_feature_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`feature_id`) REFERENCES `feature` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `payment` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `account_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `amount` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  `currency` varchar(3) NOT NULL,\n  `payment_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `status` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `stripe_payment_id` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `payment_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`account_id`) REFERENCES `account` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
